<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Axel Oxenstierna</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1583">1583</a>–<a href="/years/1654">1654</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Axel Oxenstierna was Lord High Chancellor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> from 1612 until his death in 1654. He worked closely with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustavus-adolphus">Gustavus Adolphus</a> to reform the central administration and led Swedish policy in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a> after the king's death. He is regarded as a principal architect of the governance of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swedish-empire">Swedish Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/axel-oxenstierna.jpg" alt="Axel Oxenstierna" width="320" height="393" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Michiel Jansz. van Mierevelt · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Unknown_painter_after_M._van_Mierevelt_-_Portrait_of_Axel_Ochsenstierna.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Axel Gustafsson Oxenstierna was born on 16 June 1583 at Fånö in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uppland">Uppland</a>, the oldest of nine siblings. His parents, Gustaf Gabrielsson Oxenstierna and Barbro Axelsdotter Bielke, belonged to two ancient high noble families that had held high offices in the state and church for generations. He and his brothers studied at the universities of Rostock, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wittenberg">Wittenberg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jena">Jena</a>, and on returning in 1603 he became a valet de chambre to King Charles IX of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Oxenstierna undertook his first diplomatic mission in 1606 and joined the Privy Council during it. When Charles IX died in autumn 1611, the estates set aside the rules on age so that the 17-year-old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustavus-adolphus">Gustavus Adolphus</a> could rule, and in return the king agreed to appoint Oxenstierna Lord High Chancellor, which he became on 6 January 1612. As principal Swedish plenipotentiary he negotiated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-knared">Treaty of Knäred</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> in 1613. From 7 October 1626 he was Governor-General of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, governing from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elblag">Elbląg</a>.</p><p>After the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustavus-adolphus">Gustavus Adolphus</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lutzen-1632">Lützen</a> on 6 November 1632, the Swedish council commissioned him on 12 January 1633 as plenipotentiary legate in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>. He moved his headquarters to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a>, headed the regency council for the six-year-old Christina, wrote the Instrument of Government of 1634, and argued that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> should stay in the war after the defeat at Nördlingen. He returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stockholm">Stockholm</a> in 1636.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1643 the Privy Council decided to attack <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, a war that was largely Oxenstierna's work. It ended with the Treaty of Brömsebro, by which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> gained Gotland, Saaremaa, Jämtland, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harjedalen">Härjedalen</a> and, for thirty years, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/halland">Halland</a>. He blamed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christina-queen-of-sweden">Queen Christina</a>'s interference for the modest Swedish gains at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-westphalia">Peace of Westphalia</a>. At first he opposed her wish to abdicate, but he later helped her, and he died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stockholm">Stockholm</a> on 28 August 1654, two months after the accession of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-x-gustav">Charles X Gustav</a>.</p><p>Oxenstierna is remembered for a uniform administrative system, and the boundaries of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>'s administrative counties still largely follow those he set in the 17th century. He worked to open higher education to boys outside the noble families, which has led to his description as the father of Swedish meritocracy.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Axel Oxenstierna” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The chancellor <b>Axel Oxenstierna</b> gave the state its collegiate central government in the Instrument of Government of 1634, with counties under governors.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-sweden">Kingdom of Sweden · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Axel Oxenstierna</b>, the chancellor who governed Sweden during the minority of Queen Christina (1632–1644) after Gustavus's death, gave the state a central administration of collegiate boards (collegium) responsible for finance, war, the admiralty, justice and the chancery.</blockquote><a href="/empire/swedish-empire">Swedish Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Fånö, in what is now <a href="/countries/sweden">Sweden</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stockholm">Stockholm</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/sweden">Sweden</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=59.57,17.38&amp;year=1629" aria-label="Fånö on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Axel Oxenstierna"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4458 4985h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4458 4985h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=59.57,17.38&amp;year=1629">What was here in 1629? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
